The delivery of academic statistical collaboration resources can vary among types of
institutions and across time. In particular, this variation might occur in the manage
ment of infrastructure and the business model, the staffing model and opportunities
for staff development. In this manuscript, we present examples of these three themes
in modern academic statistical collaboration units and describe key advantages and
challenges.
KEYWORDS
biostatistics, collaboration, data science, infrastructure, statistics
1 | INTRODUCTION
The delivery of the spectrum of statistical consulting to collaboration resources varies among academic departments and institutions and can
change over time. The ways in which many academic statistical collaboration units operate have also evolved over time as the use of statistics
and statistical techniques has increased and university administrators, staffing, infrastructure and fiscal conditions have changed. Best practices
and overarching principles for organizing such statistical services have been discussed and debated for half a century (e.g., Bancroft, 1971;
Boen, 1982; Carter et al., 1986; Gibbons & Freund, 1980; Minton & Freund, 1977). More recent papers have focused specifically on biostatistical
units (e.g., Arndt & Woolson, 1991; Hanlon et al., 2022; Ittenbach & DeAngelis, 2012; Niland et al., 1995; Perkins et al., 2016; Welty et al., 2013).
Gibbons and Freund (1980) present four models for providing statistical support in an academic environment: (1) placing a subject matter
expert in an applied department, (2) providing support through a statistics department with no funding infrastructure, (3) incorporating statistical
support in physical computer labs and (4) forming a statistical consulting unit with funding and support. They recommend strongly against (1) and
(2) and suggest (3) in place of (4) if funding is not available. Their suggestions are consistent with those of Welty et al. (2013) and Vance (2015),
among others, although these and other recent work (see, e.g., Wickes, 2021) discuss placing units in partnership with campus-level infrastructure
like research computing programming or Libraries more broadly as opposed to physical computer labs. Additionally, as computing becomes less
expensive and more easily accessible, physical computer labs within academic institutions may be sunsetting.
Several consistent themes across all consulting and collaboration literature are still relevant today. These include organizing infrastructure
and coordinating budget support; managing and supporting staff, personnel and students; and staying up to date with current (bio)statistical and
data science techniques through continuing education and professional development.
