Here, we provide a characterization of the current landscape of statistical resource and collaboration cores in the United States in a modern,
data-driven era in three specific themes: organizing infrastructure and fiscal and budget management; managing and supporting personnel, includ
ing faculty, staff and students; and providing continuing education and professional development so that personnel stay up to date with current
(bio)statistical and data science techniques and, if relevant, are able to advance their own research. We also recognize that there is not a one-size
fits-all approach for the consideration of these themes and that differences in missions, institutional infrastructures and programmes, among other
things, may drive individual structures. The content of this article is derived from the authors' experiences running statistical collaboration centres
at a variety of institutions of higher education, including public and private, as well as land-grant institutions. The programmes specifically
discussed are Virginia Tech's Center for Biostatistics and Health Data Science; Duke University's Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design
(BERD) Methods Core; Duke University School of Medicine's Data Science and Statistics Lab (DSSL); NC State University's Department of
Statistics, Data Science Academy and NC State Libraries' Data & Visualization Services; Purdue University's Statistical Consulting Service; and
Virginia Tech's Statistical Applications and Innovations Group (SAIG). The case study approach that we use to highlight various programmes allows
for a deep investigation into the similarities and differences of experiences among statistical collaboration types. It also lays the groundwork for
future studies of a larger sampling of programmes.
2 | INFRASTRUCTURE AND FISCAL AND BUDGET
Statistical consulting units, sometimes called cores, centres, institutes or laboratories, exist in different locations within a university infrastructure.
Some units exist in academic departments or schools while others may exist as a separate unit in the Office of the Provost or the Office for the
Vice President for Research. Statistical consulting units within land-grant institutions largely arose from agricultural research with funding from
Agricultural Experiment Stations (e.g., Virginia Tech SAIG, NC State University and Purdue University) and may still be located within the College
of Agriculture (e.g., the programme at Louisiana State University). Statistical consulting units within medical campuses may exist within the School
of Public Health, School of Medicine or a Cancer Research Institute. They may even be housed within a statistics or biostatistics department while
working primarily with a different campus unit (e.g., Virginia Tech's Center for Biostatistics and Health Data Science is academically housed within
the Department of Statistics at Virginia Tech while physically located on the Health and Technology campus). Different administrative structures
result in various challenges and opportunities. For example, a statistical collaboration unit existing in a department may not have the same access
to resources (e.g., staff and funding) as a unit existing within an upper administrative unit. Staff within a statistical collaboration unit in a depart
ment, however, may have easier access to others with expertise needed for particular client collaborators than staff within a core in an administra
tive unit. Examples of funding models, with their benefits and challenges, are described below.
2.1 | High-level administrative support in full
We define high-level administrative support in full as dedicated funding from the Office of the Vice Provost (or President) for Research, the
Provost, the Library, Institutional Research or other non-academic units. Full high-level administrative support units are centralized and tend to
benefit from stability due to consistent and dedicated hard funding. This structure frees personnel to concentrate on value-adding work
(i.e., study design, analysis, interpretation, publications, proposal writing and training of students) and can allow for flexibility in hiring compared
with being inside a specific department or academic unit. Challenges associated with this type of support include a possible lack of technical and
methodological support if there is no deeper connection between consulting and collaborative personnel and (bio)statistics or data science faculty.
Depending on the roles of involved personnel, professional development can also be difficult to coordinate. Finally, limited budget control can
leave programmes vulnerable to changes in university administration and priorities.
2.2 | Support from colleges
Support from colleges involves negotiating an amount of funding from a variety of colleges and academic units. This funding is often related to
the amount of support those colleges anticipate receiving from the statistical collaboration unit. The funding may also be tied to a particular staff
member devoted to statistical collaboration with that particular college. There are many benefits to this kind of support, including developing a
programme focused on specific colleges that need certain expertise. This model can also facilitate strong collaborative relationships with the
supporting colleges.
There are drawbacks specific to this funding model as well. Students, staff and faculty from non-participating Colleges may find themselves
without access to (bio)statistical and data science expertise. Colleges may choose not to contribute and, instead, hire their own statisticians,
distributing effort and creating redundancies. Additionally, supporting colleges may find the financial model of non-supporting colleges attractive
