2.3 | Core support from National Institutes
One example of core support from national institutes is a Clinical and Translational Science Award (CTSA) from the National Institutes for Health
(NIH). Administrators often associate these awards with prestige and recognition for the institution. The structure, duration and amount of
national institute awards allow units to flourish as they foster close collaborative and interdisciplinary relationships between subsets
of researchers. Technical competencies in areas aligned with the research direction of the award are supported by award funding. These awards
also allow bandwidth for short-term consulting for unfunded projects and for expertise from a wide variety of faculty to be involved.
Sustainability is a key challenge of National Institutes funding. For example, the CTSA funding mechanism allows for multi-year funding
(e.g., 7 years). A new proposal must be submitted to continue providing resources to collaborators at the end of the performance period. This pro
posal for continued funding may or may not be approved by the agency. Working with subsets of researchers can create narrow subject matter
and technical foci. With this model, problems can arise if funding is discontinued or if researchers that are not a part of the scope of the award
desire support or collaboration.
2.4 | Supports from grants, external contracts and fee-for-service contracts
The final model we discuss involves obtaining funding through providing collaborative or consulting work on grants and external contracts, either
as effort-based or fee-for-service personnel. Funding of this type is fairly stable and self-sustaining. Under this paradigm, collaborators are free to
pursue strategic efforts that line up with available expertise in comparison with the models presented previously. Additionally, the variety of
projects involved can facilitate professional development among personnel.
There are challenges to this funding model, as well. Positions that are entirely dependent on soft-money or fee-for-service can be difficult to
fill and sustain, leading to hiring being a challenge. Smaller grants and contracts may be difficult to work with as well, as they require personnel
to work on a large number of projects at the same time. There may be no incentive for tenure-track faculty to work on collaborative projects that
do not result in methods development because this type of work may not be meaningful for their career advancement, which could lead to the
unavailability of qualified resources and faculty for contractual effort. Early-stage investigators and researchers in underfunded research areas
may also have a difficult time succeeding in this model as it can take time to build up funding and there may not be funding to support for work in
underfunded areas. Finally, this model requires a large administrative burden with marketing, contract set-up, billing rates and time tracking,
subject to the institution's policies and procedures governing these areas.
2.5 | Hybrid models
In the current landscape, many programmes have a mix of support. A key benefit to diverse funding streams is that funding fluctuations can be
accommodated more effectively. Other benefits include a higher degree of autonomy, a wider pool of collaborative opportunities and increased
skill acquisition through exposure to a diverse set of projects.
A key challenge to the hybrid business model is that while the uncertainty that comes with soft funding is decreased, it is not eliminated.
Hybrid models still rely, sometimes heavily, on the success of specific projects. This uncertainty makes long-term planning for resources difficult.
Other challenges include the exhaustion (or burnout) that comes with continually competing for (sometimes many) opportunities with low funding
lines, along with the adaptability and flexibility required to navigate such a complex landscape, and the administrative burden that comes with
business management and planning. A knowledgeable leader who understands these complexities is a critical component of a hybrid model.
