establishment of a network within and across academic institutions, as well as collaborations with experts in various fields.
At the same time, the associated workload requires team members to juggle competing deadlines and responsibilities for various research
studies and to respond to a diverse set of personalities who serve as principal investigators. Communicating complex statistical concepts to inves
tigators from different disciplines requires effective communication skills, both written and verbal, and the ability to adapt to various research
contexts. Staff can often find themselves with limited autonomy in shaping research agendas, as principal investigators and funding opportunities
typically drive research priorities. These complexities contribute to the variety of statistical consulting unit staffing. While these are presented
individually, many modern statistical collaboration units have a mixture of staffing types.
3.1 | Statistical collaboration unit administrators and operational staff
Statistical collaboration units typically have a primary contact, a director, who manages the unit. The director is generally a faculty member
(see Hanlon et al., 2022) with an appointment in a home department (e.g., Statistics, Biostatistics and Nursing). Directors are the leaders of the
unit that pursue value-adding (i.e., billable or sponsored programme) and value-enabling (i.e., proposals and relationship building) opportunities.
Statistical collaboration units may also have a manager, assistant director or administrative professional that supports the director in terms of
addressing client/consultant disagreements, assigning appropriate consultants to projects aligned with their skill sets and available time and hiring.
The minimum degree requirement for a manager level position is typically a master's degree in a quantitative field and/or relevant experience.
Their time may be split among other job responsibilities outside of the consulting unit (e.g., 0.5 FTE in the consulting unit and 0.5 FTE teaching).
When directors are relieved of the burden of daily operations, they can focus on more strategic efforts such as managing and developing
staff, pursuing funding opportunities, managing large projects and advocating for the unit to university administrators. The manager role requires
a variety of skill sets to succeed, including basic accounting, navigating sponsored programme systems, compiling standard operating procedures
and policies and managing websites and client intake systems assignment. A business manager role may also be needed in order to have a position
fully dedicated to fiscal management, including pre-award and post-award, contracts with industry, government and other academic partners, as
well as internal collaborative partners. Creating an attractive position and finding an individual whose skills and interest align with such a position
is extremely challenging. Hiring and retaining this individual is further complicated by the inability of academia to compete with industry or private
statistical consulting business salaries. Additional staffing needs often include a bookkeeper or fiscal officer. In a programmatic model with
contracts and grants, this type of administrative support is invaluable.
3.2 | Faculty staff
Statistical collaboration units may hire dedicated faculty, may employ faculty as part of the percentage of their appointment or may collaborate
with faculty on an as-needed basis. Faculty are leading experts in the fields of data science and statistics which allow consulting units to tap into
this talent pool. Individuals in faculty positions generally also have a teaching component associated with their position. Faculty on the tenure
track have the advantage of stability, a clear path for career advancement and can be included on grants as a senior investigator (e.g., PI). Tenure
track faculty positions are often more highly valued in academia. These positions also often come with funding for students and their research as
well. Exposure to collaborative projects can facilitate methods development and research. However, obtaining tenure and advancing in the faculty
ranks can be challenging with a collaborative resume (e.g., with limited methods development and research products). Faculty who are jointly
appointed in the statistical consulting unit and an academic department must be given bandwidth to address collaborative projects through, for
example, teaching or course releases.
Like tenure-track faculty, non-tenure-track faculty also generally have a clear path for career advancement and can be included on grants as a
senior investigator (e.g., PI). However, these faculty positions may have less stability and limited term contracts (e.g., 3-year contracts). The
requirements for advancement may include a research component, in which case faculty may have to advocate that their collaborative publica
tions can fulfil this requirement. Non-tenure-track faculty are often not as highly valued in academic departments as evidenced by limited voting
rights and advancement requirements being more vague. Non-tenure-track faculty may be harder to retain given all of these aspects.
3.3 | Research associate/scientist staff
Research staff positions are similar to non-tenure-track faculty in terms of their stability and limited term contracts. These positions may have
shorter term contracts than non-tenure-track faculty. Individuals in research staff positions generally do not teach. A key advantage of this type
