The career advancement and development of individuals in research staff positions can often be vague, leading to limited opportunities for
growth. For example, some institutions may have advancement opportunities from the title research assistant to research associate or from
research associate I to research associate II. One criterion to advance from one level to the next may be that the staff member be able to work
independently, where independence is judged by the supervisor. This subjective judgement may not be applied consistently and may lead to ineq
uities in the promotion process. Salaries for these positions may also not be competitive with the statistics and data science market. Additionally,
retention in these positions may be impacted by the team members' understanding and commitment to this type of position. For these and other
reasons, research staff can be hard to retain.
3.4 | Student staff
Training the next generation of applied, collaborative statisticians is a key part of the academic mission of any programme. Students can be
involved in statistical consulting through formal coursework (possibly offered by statistical consulting faculty) or through their involvement in con
sulting through the statistical consulting unit. Students contribute refreshing creativity and cutting-edge problem-solving and methods. Addition
ally, students can develop long-term relationships with collaborators that extend beyond their studies at the institution. They may also gain
valuable experiences in writing publications with their collaborators before writing their own methods papers. Students are relatively lower cost
than full-time researchers, though in some cases, tuition and stipend might also be involved in student funding.
There are also challenges that exist with student staff. Students have the sole objective to obtain their degree and leave; thus, attrition is
expected with these staff members. Student staff may also be a limited commodity in a department with fewer students. Statistical consulting unit
student salaries may also not be competitive with research stipends from other opportunities that could directly contribute to a student's degree
progress. Unlike funding for methods research, students may not be funded to complete both their collaborative projects and their own research.
The quality of student work and the amount of supervision students may need also depend on experience, training and communication and tech
nical skill sets.
