3.6 | Professional development
Another vital aspect of keeping a (bio)statistical or data science consulting and collaboration programme relevant is professional development of
the people staffing the programme. Cutting-edge research often involves modern statistical methods, and a programme that does not allow con
tinuing education and professional development for its consultants risks burnout, stagnation and irrelevance.
3.7 | Connections to the campus (bio)statistical community
A benefit of programming on a university campus is access to research and researchers on that campus. Collaborative and consulting programmes
can use their connections to the research community on campus to provide consultations and advising on complex projects. Various campus
based seminar series are another way for researchers to keep abreast of new statistical methodological developments. For example, the statistical
collaborator can attend and participate in the seminar series in Microbiology to learn more about the methods used in that field. Incorporating
graduate students is a third way of ensuring that up-to-date methodologies are used in collaborative and consulting projects. Developing short
courses and workshops on special topics that are not part of the core curriculum is another way to encourage cross-programme connections.
Finally, allowing statistical collaborators the time to read current literature or to organize a reading group or regular peer discussion meetings can
help them maintain their awareness of current methodologies.
3.8 | Connections to the national (bio)statistical community
If sufficient budget allocation is available, programme staff can attend and participate in short courses and conferences to help them learn newer
methodologies and stay up to date on current best practices. Conference travel builds professional relationships and networks, allowing for cross
pollination of ideas and trends in methodology.
3.9 | Institution case study: Professional development
Table 3 summarizes the approaches of the case studies included in this manuscript to ensuring professional development. All units support short
course and conference attendance within budget constraints. Many units host and co-host various seminar series on translational research,
emerging topics and best practices. Both Duke units offer formal mentorship programmes for career development, and the Purdue unit includes
faculty oversight for graduate student worker projects. The NC State programme includes reading current literature for weekly discussions, while
Virginia Tech's SAIG hosts a shared drive of resources.
4 | DISCUSSION
There is not a one-size-fits-all approach for the consideration of the three themes presented in this manuscript. A statistical consulting unit's mis
sion, the infrastructure of the institution and programmes, among other things, may impact individual structures, staffing and staff professional
development opportunities. However, considering these three themes when developing or sustaining a (bio)statistical or data science consulting
programme is critical. Programmes that do not address each theme appropriately may struggle to sustain themselves. Salient points to highlight
include assessing needs, flexibility and advocacy.
The in-depth examination of a variety of programmes in this paper may be used to develop a targeted survey questionnaire to illuminate the
wider field. Identifying these three main themes is an essential first step in understanding and sustaining these critical statistical collaboration
efforts in higher education.
Whether developing a new statistical collaboration unit or leading an established unit, understanding and documenting institutional needs is
crucial for ongoing strategic planning and stakeholder commitment of funding and advocacy support. This needs assessment may include
