Adaptability is a key aspect for the success of a statistical collaboration unit. Science, institutional leadership, perceptions of value, statistical
methods and tools are dynamic. Infrastructure, staffing models and professional development opportunities of 20 years ago or even last year may
not be currently sustainable or reasonable. For example, institutional leaders may decide to shift all administration-level funding to college-level
funding. This shift in resources may lead to examining how statistical consulting efforts are comparable with the amount of funding received and
an implementation of different staffing. Subsequently, staff may need additional professional development opportunities to support working in
different areas and to reduce burnout. Leadership who understands these complexities is critical.
A plan for succession is another key aspect for the success of a statistical collaboration unit. A truly stable programme will continue to suc
ceed even with changes or transitions in the unit's director position. Institutional administrators should establish a timeline to evaluate the direc
